---
title: "Working with Buffer"
---

```{r, include = FALSE}
knitr::opts_chunk$set(
  collapse = TRUE,
  comment = "#>"
)
```

```{r setup}
library(promoutils)
library(dplyr)
```

Using the Buffer API to schedule messages.
First make sure you are authorized to work with the Buffer API:

```{r}
keys_check()
```

If not, see the [Setting Up Buffer](article/buffer_app) article.

Once you have set up your Buffer access, you can use the Buffer API to access
the rOpenSci social media accounts on Buffer using httr2 directly or via the promoutils helper functions.


### Posting messages

With Buffer you can post messages immediately (`when = "now"`) or in schedule
them for a future posting (`when = DATE TIME`). By default, new posts are set
as drafts (`draft = TRUE`) and the Buffer web app will open in your browser so
you can preview the posts if you like.

If you set `draft = FALSE`, posts scheduled for `now` will be posted *immediately*, so use caution! Scheduled posts will still be scheduled, but will be posted if you make no changes.

```{r}
buffer_posts_write("Testing a draft post!", when = "now")
```

We can get a list of posts:
```{r}
buffer_posts_list()
```

By default this only returns posts with the status of "scheduled" or "draft". 
To return posts which have been sent, change `status = "sent"`. 

```{r}
buffer_posts_list(status = "sent")
```

And we can clean up our test posts with:

```{r}
m <- buffer_posts_list() |>
  dplyr::filter(text == "Testing a draft post!")

buffer_posts_remove(m$id)
```

Check that they're gone
```{r}
buffer_posts_list() |>
  dplyr::filter(text == "Testing a draft post!")
```


### Scheduling messages

Let's schedule a message for tomorrow. 

```{r}
slack_posts_write(
  "Testing scheduled messages in the Vignette!",
  when = Sys.Date() + lubridate::days(1)
)
```

- If `when = "now"`, `slack_posts_write()` will post immediately (default)
- If scheduled, `slack_posts_write()` will also post a copy of the message and 
it's date to scheduled in the private channel, `#admin-scheduled`, 
for quick checking of currently scheduled messages when you're in the Slack app.

![Screenshot of a Slack message in the #admin-scheduled channel giving the details of the scheduled message](slack_admin_scheduled.png)

See the currently scheduled messages according to the Slack API
```{r}
slack_scheduled_list()
```


To delete currently scheduled messages you need to know the `channel_id` and the message `id`. 
We can get these from `slack_list_scheduled()` or we can supply the data frame directly as we did above.

```{r}
m <- slack_scheduled_list()
slack_scheduled_rm(m)
```

Note that `slack_scheduled_rm()` also removes the message posted to the `#admin-scheduled`
channel, so that channel *should* be a faithful representation of messages currently 
scheduled via the API.

```{r}
slack_scheduled_list()
```

However, after posts have been sent, you'll need to clean up `#admin-scheduled`
with a call to `slack_cleanup()` which will remove any message in `#admin-scheduled` that isn't also in Slack's internal scheduled messages list.

```{r}
slack_cleanup()
```